  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 243 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance shows troughing moving in at 500-mb \nthroughout the day Saturday, which would be associated with a \nsurface low in Eastern portions of Colorado. Southeasterly winds\nare favored to be in place across the area due to this surface \nlow, with NBM guidance suggesting these winds will sustain \nbetween 15 and 20 MPH, and gust to around 30 MPH. Surface high \ntemperatures are currently forecast in the low to mid 50s in \nspite of the strong southeasterly flow expected throughout the \nday. This would be driven by a 75-90% chance of cloud cover \nabove 50% based on LREF guidance, alongside a strong surface \nhigh in the Midwest recirculating cool continental air into the \nregion. \n\nGoing into the evening hours Saturday, the likelihood for \nprecipitation begins to increase, with PoPs (probability for \nprecipitation) into Sunday morning up around 60% in some places.\nNBM 24 hr precipitation guidance shows between a 50 and 65% \nchance that greater than 0.1 inches of precipitation falls \nacross the forecast area between Saturday evening and Sunday \nevening. Rain is very likely to be the precipitation type, as \nLREF guidance shows over a 90% chance that low temperatures will\nnot fall to freezing during this time period. Additionally, all\nmembers in the 00Z run of the LREF clusters on November 11 show\nno wintry precipitation occurring, an improvement from recent \nruns. Activity is forecast to remain widespread rather than \nconvective, as CAPE (a measure of atmospheric instability) \nvalues from the LREF show no more than a few hundred J/kg in the\nbest scenario. Highs on Sunday are forecast in the mid 40s to \nlower 50s, with no hazards expected. \n\nGEFS and EC ensembles are in good agreement that ridging will \nbe entering the area Monday, as troughing once again moves into \nthe Western United States off the Pacific Coast. Highs are \nforecast to return to around normal Monday and Tuesday (50s), \nwith winds set to increase, particularly on Tuesday, as the \ntrough approaches. NBM guidance suggests that winds on Tuesday \nhave at least a 40% chance of sustaining above 20 MPH and \ngusting above 30 MPH across nearly the entire forecast region. \nPrecipitation is not forecast again until Tuesday night, though \n24 hr precipitation guidance only shows a 10-20% chance for \ngreater than 0.1 inches. However, this will depend on the track \nand timing of the trough and surface low, which is relatively \nuncertain at this range. When looking at LREF members, ENS and \nGEPS members favored a more northern track, while GEFS favored a\nlow coming through the Central Plains, which would be more \nsupportive of precipitation in the area. As such, a northern \ntrack is currently favored at around 60%.",
